      "detail": "The papaya is native to tropical America and reached South China by sea in the late Ming; Lingnan calls it 「万寿果」, and because the tree is upright and bears heavily it was widely planted in courtyards. The 「番」 in its name records where it came from — it arrived on the same ships as the crops of the New World and put down roots in South China along the maritime trade routes. Over several hundred years it became the least demanding fruit tree of the south: a single upright trunk, fruit in the first year, a few planted in front of the house and behind it, and the family's fruit dish never empty through the year. At the old trading wharves of Lingnan the papaya came and went with the merchant ships, and today it is sold year-round even in northern supermarkets — the 「百益果王」 that everyone can afford at the table. From tropical America to the slopes of South China and on to thousands of homes, the migration of this tree is a maritime natural history in itself."
