      "detail": "The star fruit carries a clear dietary warning: it must not be eaten by anyone with impaired kidney function, and even healthy people should not eat a lot of it on an empty stomach. The warning bears stating more plainly: the star fruit contains a natural substance of its own, and those with damaged or reduced kidney function should avoid it altogether — a patient with kidney disease should not touch a single piece; healthy people too should not eat much on an empty stomach, and a taste is where it stops. When giving star fruit to a child, trim the ribs and remove the seeds first, and cut it into small star-shaped pieces to share. Traditional food wisdom holds that the star fruit promotes fluid and quenches thirst, and one or two slices after a summer meal are just right, with more being of no benefit. However good-looking the fruit, it must be eaten with understanding — safety always comes first on the fruit dish."
