      "detail": "The kumquat is native to southern China and has a long history of cultivation; Song-dynasty notes record that it was once common in the markets, a small fruit set before guests in winter wine shops, and that candied kumquats were a classic of the old preserved-fruit houses. Ouyang Xiu's 《归田录》 records kumquats travelling all the way from Jiangxi to Bianjing, at first recognised by nobody, then taken up by the palace too, so that their standing and price rose at a stroke — a small fruit's journey to the capital, told vividly by a Song writer. Eating it skin and all is the kumquat's own way: the skin is sweeter and more fragrant than the flesh, and this \"no peeling\" habit is unusual among fruit trees. The candying craft followed, moving from market stalls into the old preserved-fruit shops, and it has kept a place in the winter's sweet things ever since. Over a thousand years the kumquat has remained the smallest and brightest note of gold on a winter fruit dish."
