      "detail": "In producing areas such as Rong'an the kumquat hangs on the tree through winter and tastes better after frost; the growers say 「打了霜的金桔才够甜」. What the kumquat fears most in winter is a heavy frost: as soon as a frost warning comes, the growers are out in the orchard at night lighting smudge piles and hauling up covering nets, and only when the frost has melted at dawn do they pack up and go in. Through the days of keeping watch, the household that guards its orchard carefully has clean fruit in spring and gets a better price by a good margin. The main harvest comes before the New Year, when everyone in the village, young and old, goes into the orchard; the small fruit is snipped off string by string, spread in shallow trays to air and then boxed. A box of kumquats posted to sons and daughters far away goes with the words \"plenty of frost at home this year, they are sweet\" — and the New Year taste of home travels north and south like that."
