      "detail": "Small shears for cutting stalks and shallow trays for sorting mean that harvesting kumquats turns on care; frost nets and small orchard windbreaks are the winter equipment. The fruit is small and the skin thin, so every tool serves the idea of fineness: the small shears cut close to the stalk and leave half a fruit's length of stem, the shallow tray spreads the fruit in one layer, and at the sorting bench each is cut and graded at once, with damaged and split fruit thrown out on the spot. Frost equipment must be ready before winter — the nets rolled and hung at the orchard edge, the brushwood for the smudge piles stacked at the field head, so that a change in the wind can be met at once. The small windbreaks stand in rows on the northern side, and what they block is not a gale but the frost of one night. Potted kumquats and orchard kumquats are managed separately: the ornamental ones for shape, the eating ones for sugar, each going its own way."
