      "detail": "In winter folk households infuse candied kumquats in water for the throat, for the sake of that mild sweetness. On a winter night with a tight throat, two honeyed kumquats in warm water go down with the fragrance of the peel mixed into the sweet-sour, more reassuring than a lozenge — traditional food wisdom regards this as a gentle way to soothe the throat, with no claim to medical effect, only comfort. The fresh fruit is eaten skin and all, and rubbing it clean in warm water is the task before eating, so that the sweet fragrance of the peel can be trusted; the seeds are small and can simply be spat out. Candied kumquats are high in sugar, so anyone watching their sugar should only taste them, and with children agree the number in advance — a few a day. Eating many kumquats dries the mouth, and taken slowly with plain water alongside they sit most comfortably."
