      "detail": "The sugarcane stall on a winter street and the omen of 「节节高」 in wedding custom give this sweet cane a solid presence in southern folk life. On a southern festival table a pair of canes with their leaves still on is commonly set upright at either side of the fruit dish, taking the meaning 「有头有尾、节节高升」; at festival time in southern Fujian the cane is even boiled with 长年菜, securing both sweetness and long life at once. It is often among the offerings to the kitchen god as well, said to sweeten his mouth so that he speaks well in heaven. A sugarcane seller in the street is part of the winter scene: the vendor peels and chops with a flourish like a performance, and a child carries a length away, gnawing as he goes and ending with hands sticky with sweet juice. From the offering table to the street, one sweet cane gives the southern winter its taste of sweetness."
