      "detail": "Cane juice is high in sugar, satisfies a craving but not thirst, and cannot stand in for plain water — a small cup a day is enough. Traditional food wisdom holds that cane juice clears heat and eases dryness, and that a cup of warm cane juice when a dry cough comes in winter makes the throat feel better; experience is experience, but cane juice is high in sugar, satisfies a craving rather than thirst, and a small cup a day is enough — it should not replace plain water either. Those with plenty of stomach acid should drink less of the strong juice, and the pleasure of an iced glass calls for restraint above all. Cane that is red inside or mouldy carries a clear risk, so the cross-section must be checked before it is drunk; children may drink the juice, but gnawing cane and spitting the fibres needs an adult standing by, and the soft, tender sweet end is the piece to choose. Sweetness must be eaten with understanding — only then does this sweet cane truly please."
